hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in book 1 of on  free choice of the will one of the  topics that Augustine addresses with his  student of odious is the relationship  between the the virtues and here he has  in mind the cardinal virtues and what he  is calling the good will and there's  going to be sort of a reciprocal  relation between these two so that the  virtues play a central role in how it is  that the goodwill is configured how the  goodwill maintains itself and he tells  us at one point this very nice formula  when his student asks well what is a  goodwill he says a will by which we do  three things and one of these is to live  virtuously live in accordance with the  virtues the other two are to live  rightly which is you know largely a  synonym here for living virtuously  although we might you know parse it out  further and then he says and a will by  which we seek to reach the height of  human wisdom and wisdom is also going to  be connected with the virtues we're not  going to talk about that too much here  because we want to focus specifically  the virtues he also tells us two other  things a little bit earlier than that in  the dialogue one of these is that  whether or not we have and use the  virtues that is something that lies  within the power of the will so it's not  as if we're just naturally prudent or  brave or just there could be some  tendencies in that direction but that's  not yet having these virtues and quite  likely whatever natural impulses we  might have due to temperament could  easily be corrupted if we're not  thinking about this in terms of the will  and its function of choosing or  committing itself or if you like  prioritizing goods over each other so  whether or not we're virtuous lies  within the power of the will he also  tells us that if we're understanding  things in the proper framework we can  see that virtue is actually more  powerful than Vice and it's preferable  to its advice now you can say well in  that case why are people ever vicious  and we'll come to that in a moment first  though we need to think about what the  virtues actually are and to those who  are familiar with the cardinal virtues  from other frameworks perhaps those of  plate inist or stoic or even epicurean  discussions of the virtues these four  will be you know familiar to them they  are prudence fortitude or bravery or  courage you could say temperance or  self-control and justice so he leads his  his students through discussing what  these actually are and so let's look at  each of these in turn e says consider  whether prudence seems to you to consist  in knowledge of what to seek and avoid  so prudence or practical wisdom  is a type of practical knowledge that we  have of what it is that we ought to  choose or select or pursue and what it  is that we ought to reject or avoid or  be averse to now you could say well it  must rely on having some knowledge about  these things and that's quite true and  you know traditionally this this virtue  has been seen as the one which helps us  to get past mere rules to think  indeterminate cases  well what's actually good for me in this  situation or what's good for a human  being in general in this situation and  so how should I act and prudence is what  what does that for us so it directs us  towards the good or it chooses between  an array of goods the greater good and  tells us to pursue that fortitude or  courage or bravery fortitude oh right in  in Latin here he picks out one  particular feature instead of looking at  some other traditionally ascribed  features of that virtue he says  fortitude is the state of the soul in  which we despise all misfortunes all bad  things that happen to us and the loss of  things not resting in our power so it's  a capacity for going on for resisting  for not giving in and there's other ways  in which fortitude has been expressed as  well  temperance plays another very important  role and here he says something quite  interesting he says temperance is that  state of soul which controls and checks  desire in regard to those things which  it is shameful to desire so  traditionally the virtue of temperance  or self-control would be looked at as  applying to say sexuality and sexual  desire or the desire for food or the  desire for intoxicating substances you  know the ancients usually talked in  terms of drinking and wine but we could  think about getting high and many other  ways as well  so temperance is is what helps us to  structure our desires and when it  encounters desires that we have for  things that are shameful to desire  things that we ought not to desire like  you know not just having sex in general  but having sex with somebody we're not  supposed to or in ways that you know are  far to say public or something like that  it would say no no don't do that  it would resist that and and say maybe I  can't stop feeling that desire but I'm  not gonna give in to it or similarly  with eating you know if if you're  getting upset because the waiter brings  out your meal and it's not so perfect  that you'll leave a wonderful review on  Yelp  you're intemperate and temperance would  say hey knock that off the food is just  fine eat it up and then get out of there  and leave a tip right instead of leaving  a bunch of complaints very common sort  of commonplace scenario just as he says  what else are we to say about justice  then that is the virtue by which each  person is given their due and of odious  the student says yeah that's what I  think about justice so justice is is  what you know leads us to giving  everybody what it is that they deserve  that could be rewards or that could be  benefits in return or that could be a  fair distribution or that might also be  punishment as well if they actually  deserve it and we're the right person to  give that now he goes on and he asks the  person who has a good will are they  going to have these virtues and he says  can we suppose that a person who  actually has a good will is not endowed  with prudence who sees that this good  that is the good will should be sought  for and everything avoided which  conflicts with it so this is specifying  prudence and saying the person who  actually has a good will will realize  that they ought to more highly  prioritize or value that goodwill above  other goods fortitude as well  is going to be important he says why  should we not grant him fortitude he  cannot love and value all highly all  these things not under our control if we  do then their love through an evil will  and he's bound to resist an evil will as  the enemy of his most precious good so  when he loses these things like say you  know you're faced with a situation where  doing the right thing is going to cost  you some pleasures or some security so  desire or fear are at play the person  who actually has fortitude is gonna say  yeah I'm gonna take the good will I'm  gonna take in this case justice over  giving in to these these desires what  about temperance so he says can we  deprive him of temperance this is the  virtue that checks passion what is so  opposed to a good will is passion hence  you can understand that the person who  loves their good will resist their  passions by every means and fights  against them  therefore they are rightly said to have  temperance and now no it's no longer  just about the Honorable but it's a bit  more you could say across-the-board  right  and then finally justice he says I don't  see how such a person can lack this if  they possess and love to possess a good  will and resist what as opposed to it  you cannot wish evil to anyone it  follows that he harms no one and this  can only be the case if he gives to  everyone his due so you know we could  raise some issues here about punishment  or resisting evil you know by say  killing people but Augustine talks about  that in other places so I'm going to  answer gloss over that here so all of  these virtues are going to be present in  any person who actually has a good will  and who values and loves that goodwill  this leads us to thinking about how  these could be lost as well if you don't  use the virtues that you have we  you get into difficult situations of  temptation or forced choice or  difficulty then that would be a sign  that one doesn't value and decide to  keep that goodwill instead one decides  for some other thing and that would be  acting in a vicious way and that's a  good way to lose these virtues and to  thereby also abandon or lose that  goodwill in the process but so long as a  person decides that now having the  goodwill is what matters most to me they  will be able to develop and use and  probably enhance and you know  consolidate you might say these four  cardinal virtues which then help them to  keep the goodwill they make those tough  decisions a little less tough maybe even  into what we sometimes facetiously call  no-brainers meaning you don't have to  think that much about it because if you  see that the goodwill is what is truly  valuable and living virtuously as part  of that you're going to want to behave  in that way  and eventually it becomes a you know as  Aristotle had said s